      "input_state": "gas-atomized powder",
      "output_state": "as-built cubes",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 2,
          "section": "2. Experimental procedure",
          "quote": "A parameter optimization study was first performed with the aim of selecting the L-PBF processing conditions rendering both the high cooling rates necessary to stabilize the quasicrystalline hardening phases and also the high density required to avoid catastrophic failure. Eight 10 × 10 × 5 mm3 cubic specimens (Fig. 1a) were built on top of inverted pyramidal supports using the eight sets of processing parameters summarized in Table 1.",
          "table": "Table 1",
          "figure": "Fig. 1a",
          "chunk_id": "11ecc66d648faaeb:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": 2,
          "section": "2. Experimental procedure",
          "quote": "The scanning velocity in pulsed laser systems is given by the equation v = PD/(ET + DT), where PD (in mm) is the point distance and represents the distance between the center of the different melting points, ET (in s) is the laser exposure time on each point and DT (in s) is the laser delay time, which is a constant value for the machine (106 s for the Renishaw AM400). The tested processing conditions included P values ranging from 325 to 375 W and v values between 1800 and 2250 mm/s (Table 1). The values of t, h, and PD were kept constant at 0.03, 0.09 and 0.09 mm, respectively.",
          "table": "Table 1",
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "11ecc66d648faaeb:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
